---
// Reboots (#417 (technique pages, part 1)): attrition kills and restarts the
// servers, one of them on an empty disk. The recorded run is
// src/runs/reboots.json; public/reboots.js draws the table of reboots from
// the run's events.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Technique from "../../../layouts/Technique.astro";
import { url } from "../../../nav";
import { excerpt, repoFile } from "../../../lib/source.mjs";
import runs from "../../../runs/reboots.json";

const attrition = excerpt(repoFile("crates/moonpool-wasm-demo/src/engine.rs"), "out.push(Chaos::Attrition {", "mode: ChaosMode::Random,");
const boot = excerpt(repoFile("crates/moonpool-wasm-demo/src/register.rs"), "let storage = ctx.storage();", "tracing::info!(version, \"server_boot\");");
---
<Technique
  title="Reboots"
  description="A process starts again with fresh memory and reads its disk. The test goes on."
  guide={[
    "part3-building/09-attrition.md",
    "part3-building/30-outages.md",
    "part2-foundations/09-process.md",
    "part2-foundations/10-workload.md",
  ]}
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>A server restarts after a deploy, a crash or a new disk. All that it had in memory is gone. It must read
    its state from its disk, and that disk can be older than its last answer, or empty. The bugs are in this
    gap: a server that forgets a write it acknowledged, or a cluster that loses a write when one server comes
    back with nothing.</p>

  <h2>The technique</h2>
  <p>A <b>process</b> is the system under test. Each boot makes a new instance from its factory, so nothing in
    memory survives a reboot. The disk survives, with the crash model of the <a href={url("techniques/storage/")}>storage page</a>. A <b>workload</b> is the test driver. It survives
    each reboot, keeps its requests going and checks the answers.</p>
  <p><b>Attrition</b> reboots the processes during the chaos time. Each reboot is <b>graceful</b> (a signal,
    a grace period, then a kill), a <b>crash</b> (a kill at once), or a <b>crash and wipe</b> (a kill, then an
    empty disk). <code>max_dead</code> is the most processes that are down at the same time, so a quorum can
    stay up. A process can also be rebooted at a moment its code names with <code>hint!</code>.</p>

  <h2>The live run</h2>
  <p>Three servers and one client. At 437 ms a server is crashed and wiped at its own hint, after it wrote a
    record and before the sync. It boots again with version 0: its disk is empty. Later a crash hits another
    server, and it boots with the version it synced. The client did not stop, and each read returned the last
    committed write.</p>
  <div class="breakout">
    <section class="run" id="run" aria-label="A live run"></section>
    <div class="run run-under">
      <div class="run-head label"><span>each reboot of a server</span></div>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table class="run-table" id="reboot-table"></table></div>
      <p class="run-out" id="reboot-out" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>
    <noscript><p>The live run needs JavaScript.</p></noscript>
  </div>
  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="runs" set:html={JSON.stringify(runs).replace(/</g, "\\u003c")} />

  <h2>The code</h2>
  <p>The attrition of the site's engine. A wipe needs a budget: a wiped server of the register has no repair,
    so one wipe at a time can stay unhealed, and each later wipe becomes a crash:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={attrition} />
  <p>Each boot of a server starts here, with empty memory. It opens its log, reads it and keeps the newest
    record that passes its check word:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={boot} />
  <script is:inline type="module" src={url("reboots.js")}></script>
</Technique>
